{"causes": ["The widespread dissemination of Greek philosophical traditions, particularly Stoicism and Pythagoreanism, throughout the Roman Republic provided a rich intellectual foundation.", "The Roman cultural emphasis on practical ethics, moral virtue, and civic duty created fertile ground for philosophies that offered guidance for living well.", "The syncretic intellectual environment of the late Republic encouraged philosophers to combine and adapt diverse philosophical tenets into new systems."], "effects": ["Quintus Sextius established the Sextian school, a distinctive philosophical movement in Rome that combined elements of Pythagoreanism and Stoicism.", "His philosophical teachings significantly influenced Seneca the Younger, who frequently cited Sextius as a moral authority and source of inspiration.", "Sextius's advocacy for specific ethical practices, such as vegetarianism and daily self-examination, contributed to the broader Roman discourse on personal morality."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "quintus-sextius", "sourceName": "Quintus Sextius", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "seneca-the-younger", "targetName": "Seneca the Younger", "context": "Quintus Sextius's moral philosophy and ethical practices significantly influenced Seneca the Younger, who frequently referenced and praised him in his writings."}, {"sourceSlug": "pythagoras", "sourceName": "Pythagoras", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "quintus-sextius", "targetName": "Quintus Sextius", "context": "The philosophy of Pythagoras, particularly its emphasis on vegetarianism and the sanctity of life, served as a foundational influence on Quintus Sextius's ethical system."}, {"sourceSlug": "quintus-sextius", "sourceName": "Quintus Sextius", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-city", "targetName": "Rome", "context": "Quintus Sextius founded and led his philosophical school primarily in the city of Rome during the late Roman Republic."}, {"sourceSlug": "quintus-sextius", "sourceName": "